---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site-config';
import { SITES, BRAND } from '../../lib/sites';
import { articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.data}/explain/test-basis-matters/`;
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Explainers', url: '/explain/' },
  { name: 'Why the test basis matters', url: '/explain/test-basis-matters/' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  articleJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    headline: 'Why the test basis matters (dry vs as-received)',
    description:
      'The same charcoal can report very different moisture, ash and fixed-carbon figures depending on whether they are stated dry or as-received. What the difference means and how it changes a purchase decision.',
    datePublished: '2026-10-07',
    dateModified: '2026-10-07',
  }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.data}/` },
    { name: 'Explainers', url: `${SITES.data}/explain/` },
    { name: 'Why the test basis matters', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Why the test basis matters: dry vs as-received"
  description="The same charcoal can report very different moisture, ash and fixed-carbon figures depending on whether they are stated dry or as-received. What the difference means and how it changes a purchase decision."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explainer</p>
      <h1>Why the test basis matters: dry vs as-received</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        "As-received" describes the material as it landed — water included. "Dry" describes it
        after moisture is removed. The same batch reports different numbers on each basis, and
        confusing the two is one of the most common ways a spec comparison goes wrong.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>What the two bases mean</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>As-received (AR)</strong> reports a value as a percentage of the sample's mass
        <em>including its water content</em> — the state in which it was sampled, shipped or
        delivered. <strong>Dry basis (db)</strong> reports the same quantity as a percentage of
        the sample's mass <em>after moisture has been driven off</em>, so water no longer dilutes
        the other components.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because water is dead weight, as-received figures for ash, volatile matter and fixed
        carbon are all slightly <em>lower</em> than their dry-basis equivalents for the same
        material — the fixed percentages are diluted by the moisture fraction.
      </p>

      <h2>A worked illustration</h2>
      <p>
        Take a charcoal with 10% moisture and, on a dry basis, 80% fixed carbon. Stated
        as-received, that fixed-carbon figure becomes 72% (80% of the remaining 90% that is not
        water). Neither number is wrong; they answer different questions. The dry-basis figure
        describes the fuel's composition, while the as-received figure describes what you
        actually bought per tonne.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it changes a purchase decision</h2>
      <p>
        If you are paying by weight, the as-received basis is the commercially honest one: it
        tells you how much of each delivered tonne is water and how much is fuel. The dry basis
        is the technically honest one: it lets you compare the fuel's intrinsic composition
        across suppliers and batches without moisture confusing the picture.
      </p>
      <p>
        A supplier quoting a dry-basis ash figure while a competitor quotes as-received can make
        an identical product look different. Ask every supplier to state the basis next to every
        percentage, and where possible to restate the whole proximate analysis on one agreed
        basis.
      </p>

      <h2>How to spot an unstated basis</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A moisture figure with no basis is ambiguous — moisture is itself usually stated as-received.</li>
        <li>Fixed carbon should reconcile with moisture, volatile matter and ash on the same basis; if the four do not sum consistently, the basis is likely mixed.</li>
        <li>A datasheet that reports ash "as-received" but fixed carbon "dry" without saying so is internally inconsistent.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-compare/">How to compare two suppliers</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-read-a-spec-sheet/">How to read a spec sheet</a></li>
        <li><a href="/specifications/moisture/">Moisture — specification methodology</a></li>
        <li><a href="/specifications/fixed-carbon/">Fixed carbon — specification methodology</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
